airwise distinct, the three lines are distinct.\n\n#### 2. Equations of the lines\n\nUsing point\u2013slope form:\n\n* **Line \\(L_{1}\\)** through \\((1,1)\\) with slope \\(1\\):\n  \\[\n  y-1 = 1\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_{1}:\\; y = x .\n  \\]\n\n* **Line \\(L_{2}\\)** through \\((1,3)\\) with slope \\(-2\\):\n  \\[\n  y-3 = -2\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_{2}:\\; y = -2x +5 .\n  \\]\n\n* **Line \\(L_{3}\\)** through \\((1,2)\\) with slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\):\n  \\[\n  y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad L_{3}:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 .\n  \\]\n\nAll three lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(1,\\,-2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\), none belonging to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n#### 3. Verification of coverage\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|----------------|----------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1+5 = 3\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2+5 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) \u2713 |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) \u2713 |\n\nThus every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the three lines.\n\n#### 4. Count of sunny lines\n\nAll three lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) are sunny, so the configuration contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nk = 3\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\n### Conclusion ###\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there exists a set of three distinct lines, all sunny, that covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\). Hence the integer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3}\n\\]\n\nis attainable for \\(n=3\\)."
