< \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 = N_n, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n* if \\(n\\) is odd, the bound is tight and \\(k=n\\) is not ruled out;\n* if \\(n\\) is even, the bound is strict, so at least one line must be\n  non‑sunny. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ \\text{even}).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Families of sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor any integer \\(d\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; a-b=d\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y=x-d\\text{)} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_d\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(L_d\\) iff \\(a=b+d\\) and  \n\n\\[\n2b+d\\le n+1,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\\qquad b+d\\ge 1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (6) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on \\(L_d\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\big|L_d\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\big|=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Big\\rfloor, & d\\ge 0,\\\\[3mm]\n\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+d}{2}\\Big\\rfloor, & d<0 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.1  All lines non‑sunny  (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and hence lies on\n\\(y=b\\).  All these lines are non‑sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\n*Full sunny set (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nChoose the \\(n\\) values  \n\n\\[\nd=-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m .\n\\]\n\nThe corresponding lines \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-m}^{m}\\) are \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(-m\\le a-b\\le m\\); thus\n\\((a,b)\\) belongs to exactly one of these lines, and the whole set\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.  Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n*Intermediate values of \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)).*  \nFix \\(k\\) and keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{-m},L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{-m+k-1}.\n\\]\n\nThe points not covered by them have second coordinate \\(b\\) belonging to\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  \nReplace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \nEvery uncovered point lies on its appropriate horizontal line, so the\nconfiguration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for odd \\(n\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 4.3  Even \\(n=2m\\)\n\n*Maximum sunny set (\\(k=n-1\\)).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{-m+1},L_{-m+2},\\dots ,L_{m}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) one checks that the union of the lines in (8) covers all points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) **except** the \\(m\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(b-m,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\n      =\\{(1,1+m),(2,2+m),\\dots ,(m,2m)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdd the single horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\); it contains the point\n\\((1,m+1)\\) from (9) and also contains all points \\((a,m+1)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  The remaining points of (9) have second coordinate\n\\(b\\in\\{m+2,\\dots ,2m\\}\\); each such \\(b\\) already appears as the\n\\(y\\)-coordinate of one of the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) that we\nuse in the next step, so they are also covered.  \nConsequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (8) together with the horizontal line\n\\(y=m+1\\) cover the whole \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n*Intermediate values of \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).*  \nFix \\(k\\) and keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{-m+1},L_{-m+2},\\dots ,L_{-m+k}.\n\\]\n\nReplace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \nEvery point not lying on the chosen sunny lines has its second coordinate\nin \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and is therefore covered by the appropriate\nhorizontal line.  Thus the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\nTherefore for even \\(n\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for an even \\(n\\) there exists a configuration\nwith \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nBy (1) each line contains at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor=n/2\\) points of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\); hence the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\), a contradiction.\nThus no configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists when \\(n\\) is even, confirming (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructions of Sections 4.2 and 4.3 with the impossibility\nproved in Section 5 yields the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: }  k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: } k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
