ase construction (all non\u2011sunny):*  \n     \u2013 Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n     \u2013 Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  \n     \u2013 All these lines are non\u2011sunny, giving \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Adding sunny lines:*  \n     \u2013 For any integer \\(d\\) define the line  \n       \\[\n         L_d:\\; a-b=d\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y=x-d\\text{)} .\n       \\]\n       Its slope is \\(1\\), hence it is sunny.  \n     \u2013 The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(L_d\\) are exactly  \n       \\[\n         \\{(b+d,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;b+d\\ge 1,\\;2b+d\\le n+1\\}.\n       \\]\n       Their number is \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) when \\(d\\ge 0\\) and\n       \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+d}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) when \\(d<0\\).\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2m+1\\):*  \n     \u2013 Choose the \\(n\\) values \\(d=-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\).  \n     \u2013 The family \\(\\{L_d\\}_{d=-m}^{m}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n     \u2013 A direct verification (by the description above) shows that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\) in this range; hence the \\(n\\) lines cover all points.  \n     \u2013 Consequently \\(k=n\\) is attainable.  \n     \u2013 To obtain any smaller \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)), keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{-m},L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{-m+k-1}\\) and replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n       Every point not covered by the chosen sunny lines has its second coordinate \\(b\\) in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and is therefore covered by the corresponding horizontal line.  \n       Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) in the stated range.\n\n   *Even \\(n=2m\\):*  \n     \u2013 Take the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_{-m+1},L_{-m+2},\\dots ,L_{m}\\) (all values of \\(d\\) except \\(d=-m\\)).  \n       Their total number of covered points is  \n\n       \\[\n         \\sum_{d=-m+1}^{m}\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{2m+1-d}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n         =\\frac{2m(2m+1)}2-m\n         =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-m .\n       \\]\n\n       The uncovered points are precisely  \n\n       \\[\n         \\{(b-m,b)\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\n         =\\{(1,1+m),(2,2+m),\\dots ,(m,m+m)\\},\n       \\]\n\n       i.e. exactly \\(m=n/2\\) points, each lying on a distinct horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\in\\{m+1,\\dots ,2m\\}\\).\n\n     \u2013 Add one non\u2011sunny horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\).  \n       This line contains the point \\((1,m+1)\\) and, because \\(m+1\\le n\\), also contains all points \\((a,m+1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); in particular it covers the missing point \\((1,m+1)\\).  \n       The remaining missing points \\((2,m+2),\\dots ,(m,2m)\\) are each on a different horizontal line \\(y=m+2,\\dots ,y=2m\\), but those lines are already present among the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines we used to replace the sunny lines \\(L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{m}\\).  \n       Consequently the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together with the single horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\).  \n       Hence we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n     \u2013 As before, to obtain any smaller \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)) we keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{-m+1},\\dots ,L_{-m+k}\\) and replace the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n       The uncovered points are again those whose second coordinate lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and are therefore covered by the added horizontal lines.  \n       Thus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\).**  \n   \u2013 Suppose all \\(n\\) lines were sunny.  \n   \u2013 By the bound of Step\u202f1, the total number of points they could cover is at most  \n\n     \\[\n       n