ossible beyond those given. For odd n, k=n is possible (all lines sunny). For even n, k=n is impossible due to counting bound. The solution also shows that any intermediate k is possible via mixing sunny lines and horizontal lines. So answer seems correct.\n\nBut we need to check if there are any missing possibilities: Could there be a construction with k=n-1 for odd n? Yes, intermediate k includes that. Could there be a construction with k=n for even n using some non-horizontal lines but not all sunny? The solution says no due to counting bound. That seems correct.\n\nThus answer correctness is likely correct.\n\nNow Level 2: Reasoning validity. The solution uses counting arguments (bound on points per sunny line), uses explicit construction of lines with slope 1, uses case analysis (odd/even n), uses contradiction for impossibility of k=n when n even. All these are valid methods. The solution also uses a construction claim: \"Take the n horizontal lines y=1,...,y=n.\" That's concrete: explicit equations. For odd n full sunny set: \"Choose d = -m,...,m, lines L_d: a-b = d (i.e., y = x - d)\". That's explicit. For intermediate k: \"Fix k and keep the first k sunny lines L_{-m},...,L_{-m+k-1}. Replace the remaining n-k lines by horizontal lines y=1,...,y=n-k.\" That's concrete: specifying which lines. For even n max sunny set: \"Take n-1 sunny lines L_{-m+1},...,L_m\" and add horizontal line y=m+1. That's concrete. For intermediate k: similar.\n\nThus the construction claims have concrete details (Category C). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to identify any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution includes a counting bound argument: It says for a sunny line ℓ, the sequence of a+b values along ℓ∩P_n can increase at most floor((n+1)/2) times, thus |ℓ∩P_n| ≤ floor((n+1)/2). This is plausible but we need to check if the reasoning is rigorous. It says: \"If (a1,b1),(a2,b2) ∈ ℓ∩P_n with b2 > b1, then a2 - a1 has the same sign as 