 etc. But we need to verify existence. The solution's construction fails, but maybe there is a known construction. Let's try to find one manually for n=5.\n\nPoints: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4); (3,1),(3,2),(3,3); (4,1),(4,2); (5,1). We need 5 lines, each covering 3 points. Let's try to find lines that each contain 3 points.\n\nWe can try to use lines of slope 1: L0: a-b=0 includes (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). That's 3 points. Good. Next, slope 1 line L1: a-b=1 includes (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) but (4,3) sum=7 >6, not allowed. So L1 includes (2,1),(3,2). Only 2 points. L-1: a-b=-1 includes (1,2),(2,3),(3,4?) (3,4) sum=7 >6, not allowed. So only (1,2),(2,3). So slope 1 lines alone cannot give triples beyond L0. So we need other slopes.\n\nConsider line through (1,5) and (5,1): slope = (5-1)/(1-5) = 4/(-4) = -1, not allowed (slope -1). So not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (1,5) and (2,3): slope = (5-3)/(1-2) = 2/(-1) = -2, sunny. Does it pass through a third point? Let's see if (3,1) lies on that line: slope from (1,5) to (3,1) = (5-1)/(1-3) = 4/(-2) = -2, yes. So line L: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) are collinear with slope -2. Good. That's a triple.\n\nNow we have used points (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Also we have used (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) for L0. That's 6 points used. Remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 9 points left, need 3 lines each covering 3 points.\n\nConsider line through (1,2) and (4,1): slope = (2-1)/(1-4) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (2,5/3?) not integer. Maybe (2,? ) Not likely. Let's try other combos.\n\nConsider line through (1,4) and (4,2): slope = (4-2)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (2,? ) (2,?) such that slope from (1,4) to (2,y) = (4-y)/(1-2) = (4-y)/(-1) = y-4. For slope -2/3, we need y-4 = -2/3 => y = 4 - 2/3 = 10/3, not integer. So not.\n\nConsider line through (1,4) and (5,1): slope = (4-1)/(1-5) = 3/(-4) = -3/4, sunny. Does it contain (2,?)? For slope -3/4, we need (4 - y)/(1-2) = (4-y)/(-1) = y-4 = -3/4 => y = 4 - 3/4 = 13/4, not integer.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (1-2)/(2-4) = (-1)/(-2) = 1/2, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (3,? ) slope from (2,1) to (3,y) = (y-1)/(3-2) = y-1. For slope 1/2, need y-1 = 1/2 => y = 1.5, not integer. So no.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (5,1): slope = 0, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = (-1)/(-1) = 1, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (4,3) sum=7 >6, not allowed. So only two points.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (1,2): slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1/1 = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (2,1) and (1,3): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (3,-1) not allowed.\n\nConsider line through (2,4) and (4,2): slope = (4-2)/(2-4) = 2/(-2) = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope = (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/(-3) = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (3,2) and (5,1): slope = (2-1)/(3-5) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (4,? ) slope from (3,2) to (4,y) = (y-2)/(4-3) = y-2. For slope -1/2, need y-2 = -1/2 => y = 3/2, not integer.\n\nConsider line through (3,2) and (1,4): slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (3,2) and (2,4): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2/1 = -2, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (1,6) not allowed.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (5,1): slope 0, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (2,3): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (1,4): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (4,1) and (2,2): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (3,? ) slope from (2,2) to (3,y) = (y-2)/(3-2) = y-2. For slope -0.5, need y-2 = -0.5 => y = 1.5, not integer.\n\nConsider line through (4,2) and (5,1): slope = (2-1)/(4-5) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny.\n\nConsider line through (4,2) and (2,1): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. Does it contain a third point? Check (3,? ) slope from (2,1) to (3,y) = (y-1)/(3-2) = y-1. For slope 0.5, need y-1 = 0.5 => y = 1.5, not integer.\n\nConsider line through (5,1) and (3,2): slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Already considered.\n\nThus it's not trivial to find a partition into triples. But maybe there is a known construction using lines of slope 2 and -2 etc. Let's try to systematically find a construction for n=5.\n\nWe need 5 sunny lines covering all points. Since each line can have at most 3 points, we need exactly 5 lines each covering exactly 3 points. So we need a partition of the 15 points into 5 triples each collinear on a sunny line.\n\nWe already have one triple: (1,5),(2,3),(3,1) slope -2.\n\nAnother triple: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) slope 1.\n\nNow we have 9 points left: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Let's try to find triples among these.\n\nConsider triple (1,2),(2,4),(3,6?) not allowed. (1,2),(3,2),(5,2?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (1,2),(2,1),(3,0?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (1,2),(4,1),(7,0?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (1,4),(2,1),(3,-2?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (1,4),(3,2),(5,0?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (1,4),(4,2),(7,0?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (2,1),(4,2),(6,3?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) not allowed.\n\nConsider triple (2,1),(5,1) horizontal not sunny.\n\nConsider triple (4,1),(5,1) horizontal not sunny.\n\nConsider triple (4,1),(2,4),(0,7?) not allowed.\n\nMaybe we need to use lines of slope -1/2 or -2 etc.\n\nLet's try to find a line that contains three points among the remaining set.\n\nList remaining points with coordinates:\n\n(1,2) d=-1\n(1,3) d=-2\n(1,4) d=-3\n(2,1) d=1\n(2,4) d=-2\n(3,2) d=1\n(4,1) d=3\n(4,2) d=2\n(5,1) d=4\n\nWe need three points with same slope line.\n\nOne approach: find three points that are collinear with slope 2 (or -2). For slope 2, line equation y = 2x + c. Let's test points:\n\n- (1,2) => c = 0\n- (2,1) => c = -3 (since 1 = 2*2 + c => c = -3)\n- (3,2) => c = -4 (2 = 2*3 + c => c = -4)\n- (4,1) => c = -7\n- (5,1) => c = -9\n- (1,3) => c = 1\n- (1,4) => c = 2\n- (2,4) => c = 0 (since 4 = 2*2 + c => c = 0)\n- (4,2) => c = -6\n\nSo slope 2 lines with c=0 contain (1,2) and (2,4). That's two points. Need a third point with c=0: (3,6) not in set. So no triple.\n\nSlope -2 lines: y = -2x + c. Check points:\n\n- (1,2) => c = 4\n- (1,3) => c = 5\n- (1,4) => c = 6\n- (2,1) => c = 5\n- (2,4) => c = 8\n- (3,2) => c = 8\n- (4,1) => c = 9\n- (4,2) => c = 10\n- (5,1) => c = 11\n\nLine with c=5 contains (1,3) and (2,1). Need third point with c=5: (3,-1) not allowed. So no triple.\n\nSlope 1/2 lines: y = (1/2)x + c. Multiply by 2: 2y = x + 2c => x = 2y - 2c. Let's check integer points.\n\nBetter approach: find three points (a,b) that satisfy (b - b1)/(a - a1) = (b2 - b1)/(a2 - a1) = (b3 - b1)/(a3 - a1). Let's try to find triples manually.\n\nWe can also try to find a line with slope -1/2: y = -0.5 x + c. For integer points, we need x even to get integer y. Points with even a: a=2,4. Let's check (2,1): y = -0.5*2 + c = -1 + c => c = 2. So line y = -0.5 x + 2 passes through (2,1). Check (4,1): y = -0.5*4 + 2 = -2 + 2 = 0, not 1. So not.\n\nCheck (2,4): y = -0.5*2 + c => 4 = -1 + c => c = 5. So line y = -0.5 x + 5 passes through (2,4). Check (4,2): y = -0.5*4 + 5 = -2 + 5 = 3, not 2. So not.\n\nCheck (4,2): y = -0.5*4 + c => 2 = -2 + c => c = 4. So line y = -0.5 x + 4 passes through (4,2). Check (2,5): not allowed. Check (6,1): not allowed.\n\nSo not many triples.\n\nMaybe we need to use lines of slope 2/3 or 3/2 etc.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer for odd n is wrong? Let's test n=5 for k=5 (all sunny). We need to see if it's possible. If it's impossible, then answer is wrong. Let's try to see if any known results exist. The problem is reminiscent of covering a triangular lattice with lines. The maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is floor((n+1)/2). For n odd, floor((n+1)/2) = (n+1)/2. So each sunny line can cover at most (n+1)/2 points. With n sunny lines, total coverage capacity is n*(n+1)/2 = N_n. So the bound is tight. However, to achieve equality, each sunny line must cover exactly (n+1)/2 points, and the sets of points covered by different sunny lines must be disjoint. So we need a partition of the N_n points into n subsets each of size (n+1)/2, each subset collinear on a sunny line. This is a combinatorial design problem. Does such a partition exist for all odd n? The solution claims yes using lines of slope 1, but that fails. But perhaps there is another construction that works. Let's see if we can find a general construction for odd n.\n\nWe need to partition the triangular grid of points (a,b) with a,b ≥ 1, a+b ≤ n+1 into n lines of slope not 0,∞,-1, each line containing exactly (n+1)/2 points. For n odd, (n+1)/2 is integer. For n=3, (n+1)/2 = 2, we found a partition: lines of slopes 1, -0.5, -2 each covering two points. So for n=3, possible. For n=5, (n+1)/2 = 3. So we need lines each covering exactly 3 points. We found two lines covering 3 points each: slope 1 line (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) and slope -2 line (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). Need three more lines covering the remaining 9 points. Let's see if we can find three lines each covering 3 points among the remaining points. The remaining points are:\n\n(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,4),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1).\n\nWe need to partition these into three triples each collinear on a sunny line. Let's try to find triples.\n\nOne approach: Use slope 1 lines again: L_d for some d values. For d = -3: a-b = -3 => a = b-3. Points with b≥4: (1,4) (b=4 => a=1) and (2,5) not allowed. So only (1,4) is there, not enough.\n\nd = -2: a-b = -2 => a = b-2. Points: (1,3) (b=3 => a=1), (2,4) (b=4 => a=2). That's two points. Need third point: (3,5) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nd = -1: a-b = -1 => a = b-1. Points: (1,2), (2,3) not in remaining (2,3) used? Actually (2,3) used in slope -2 line? Wait (2,3) is used in the slope -2 line (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). So (2,3) is already used. So remaining points with a-b = -1: (1,2) only. (3,4) not allowed. So only one.\n\nd = 0: a-b = 0 => (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) used already. So none left.\n\nd = 1: a-b = 1 => a = b+1. Points: (2,1) (b=1 => a=2), (3,2) (b=2 => a=3) used? (3,2) is not used yet. Actually (3,2) is in remaining. So (2,1),(3,2). Also (4,3) not allowed. So two points.\n\nd = 2: a-b = 2 => a = b+2. Points: (3,1) used, (4,2) remaining, (5,3) not allowed. So (4,2) only.\n\nd = 3: a-b = 3 => a = b+3. Points: (4,1) remaining, (5,2) not allowed. So (4,1) only.\n\nd = 4: a-b = 4 => (5,1) remaining.\n\nThus slope 1 lines cannot give triples among remaining points.\n\nLet's try slope -1/2 lines: y = -0.5 x + c. For integer points, x must be even for y integer. Even x values among remaining: 2,4. Points with x=2: (2,1),(2,4). Points with x=4: (4,1),(4,2). Let's see if any line with slope -1/2 passes through three of these points. For slope -1/2, equation: y = -0.5 x + c => c = y + 0.5 x. Compute c for each point:\n\n(2,1): c = 1 + 1 = 2.\n(2,4): c = 4 + 1 = 5.\n(4,1): c = 1 + 2 = 3.\n(4,2): c = 2 + 2 = 4.\n\nSo each has distinct c, so no line with slope -1/2 passes through two of them, let alone three.\n\nLet's try slope -2/3 lines: y = -(2/3)x + c => 3y = -2x + 3c => 2x + 3y = 3c. For integer points, we can compute 2a + 3b. Compute for each remaining point:\n\n(1,2): 2*1 + 3*2 = 2 + 6 = 8.\n(1,3): 2 + 9 = 11.\n(1,4): 2 + 12 = 14.\n(2,1): 4 + 3 = 7.\n(2,4): 4 + 12 = 16.\n(3,2): 6 + 6 = 12.\n(4,1): 8 + 3 = 11.\n(4,2): 8 + 6 = 14.\n(5,1): 10 + 3 = 13.\n\nWe need three points with same 2a+3b value (i.e., same c). Values: 7,8,11,12,13,14,16. There are duplicates: 11 appears for (1,3) and (4,1). 14 appears for (1,4) and (4,2). 12 appears for (3,2) only. 8 appears for (1,2) only. 7 appears for (2,1) only. 13 appears for (5,1) only. 16 appears for (2,4) only. So no triple.\n\nTry slo