 \nHence for every odd \\(n\\ge5\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\nFor the smallest odd case \\(n=3\\) we have \\(\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nChoosing three distinct lines of slopes \\(1\\), \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), each passing through exactly one of the three points, gives a configuration with \\(k=3\\).  \n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn,   & n=3,\\\\[1mm]\nn-1, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4.  (Construction attaining the maximal number of sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge4\\).  Write \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n*Even case \\(n=2m\\).*  \nDefine the following sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: a-b=i \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1),\\\\\nM_j &: a+2b=2m+1+j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), the \\(M_j\\) have slope \\(-2\\); all are sunny and distinct.  \nThere are \\((m)+(m-1)=2m-1=n-1\\) such lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \n\n- If \\(a\\ge b\\) then \\(i:=a-b\\) satisfies \\(0\\le i\\le m-1\\) because \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\n- If \\(a<b\\) write \\(b=a+d\\) with \\(d\\ge1\\).  \n  From \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\) we obtain \\(2a+d\\le2m+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le m-\\frac{d-1}{2}\\).  \n  Compute \\(c:=a+2b=a+2(a+d)=3a+2d\\).  \n  Using the bound on \\(a\\) one checks that \\(c\\) belongs to the interval \\([2m+2,\\,2m+2+(m-1)]\\); therefore there exists a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) with \\(c=2m+1+j\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(M_j\\).\n\nAll points with \\(a<b\\) are covered except the point \\((1,m+1)\\), which fails the inequality \\(a\\ge1,\\;b=m+1\\) and gives \\(c=2m+3\\) outside the range of the \\(M_j\\).  \nAdding the horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\) (which is not sunny) covers this missing point and does not duplicate any previous line.  \nThus we have a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing **\\(n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n*Odd case \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).*  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: a-b=i \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,m),\\\\\nM_j &: a+2b=2m+2+j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow there are \\((m+1)+m=2m+1=n\\) sunny lines.  \nThe same verification as above shows that every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge b\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\), and every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a<b\\) lies on a suitable \\(M_j\\).  Hence for **\\(n=3\\)** (i.e. \\(m=1\\)) we obtain a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nFor \\(m\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge5\\)) the line \\(L_m\\) coincides with the non\u2011sunny line \\(y=m+1\\); consequently the number of *distinct* sunny lines in the family is only \\(n-1\\).  Thus the maximal attainable number of sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge4\\) is \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5.  (Realising every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (5).  \nStart from the configuration of Lemma\u202f4 that realises the maximal number of sunny lines (either the even\u2011\\(n\\) family (6) together with the extra horizontal line, or the special \\(n=3\\) construction).  \n\nReplace arbitrarily \\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by distinct horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \nEach replacement introduces a non\u2011sunny line; the set of horizontal lines together covers all points whose second coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not already covered by the remaining sunny lines has its \\(y\\)-coordinate in this set, and therefore is covered by one of the newly added horizontal lines.  \nThe resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThus **every integer \\(k\\) with**  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1\\quad (n\\ge4),\\qquad\\text{and also }k=n\\text{ when }n=3,\n\\]\n\n**is attainable**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f3 (upper bound) with Lemma\u202f4 and Lemma\u202f5 (constructions) we obtain the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
