ines of slopes 1, -2 and -1/2, each passing through exactly one of the three points, gives a configuration with k=3.\" Actually they need to cover all six points. But they claim each line passes through exactly one of the three points? That seems insufficient. Let's examine n=3 case more carefully.\n\nPoints for n=3: a+b ≤4. List them:\n\nSum 2: (1,1)\nSum 3: (1,2),(2,1)\nSum 4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\n\nTotal 6 points.\n\nWe need 3 lines covering all 6 points. If each line is sunny, they must be not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0. They can be any other slopes.\n\nWe need to find 3 sunny lines covering all points. Is it possible? Let's try to find lines that each contain 2 points (max capacity). Since each sunny line can contain at most floor((n+1)/2)=2 points, we need exactly 3 lines each covering 2 points. So we need to partition the 6 points into 3 lines, each line containing 2 points. Let's see if possible.\n\nWe can try lines of type a+2b=c or 2a+b=c. For n=3, s_n = floor((3+1)/2)=2. So lines attaining 2 points are those with |p+q|=1. Let's list lines with 2 points.\n\nCompute a+2b=c for c from 2 to 2n=6.\n\nc=2: none.\nc=3: (1,1) only.\nc=4: (2,1) only.\nc=5: (3,1) and (1,2) => 2 points.\nc=6: (4,1) not allowed (a+b=5>4), (2,2) and (0,3) not allowed => (2,2) only? Actually a+2b=6 => b=1 => a=4 not allowed; b=2 => a=2 => (2,2) sum=4 allowed; b=3 => a=0 not allowed. So only (2,2). So only c=5 yields 2 points: (3,1) and (1,2). Good.\n\nNow 2a+b=c lines:\n\nc=2: none.\nc=3: (1,1) only.\nc=4: (1,3) only? Let's compute: 2a+b=4 => a=1 => b=2 => (1,2) sum=3; a=2 => b=0 not allowed. So (1,2) only. Actually (1,2) appears again.\n\nc=5: 2a+b=5 => a=1 => b=3 => (1,3) sum=4; a=2 => b=1 => (2,1) sum=3. So (1,3) and (2,1) are 2 points.\n\nc=6: 2a+b=6 => a=1 => b=4 => (1,4) sum=5 >4 not allowed; a=2 => b=2 => (2,2) sum=4; a=3 => b=0 not allowed. So only (2,2).\n\nThus we have lines with 2 points: a+2b=5 gives (3,1),(1,2); 2a+b=5 gives (1,3),(2,1). That's 4 points covered. Th