,3) sum=7 invalid; b=4 => a=2 => (2,4) sum=6 ok; b=5 => a=0 invalid. So one point. c=11: b=5 => a=1 => (1,5) sum=6 ok; b=4 => a=3 => (3,4) sum=7 invalid; b=3 => a=5 => (5,3) sum=8 invalid. So one point. So max points on a+2b=c is 3 (c=7). So indeed max is 3 for n=5, which matches floor((n+1)/2) = 3. So Lemma 1 seems plausible.\n\nBut is the bound always floor((n+1)/2)? Let's test n=4. Points: sums from 2 to 5 inclusive (4 values). floor((n+1)/2) = floor(5/2) = 2. So claim: any sunny line can contain at most 2 points. Let's test line a+2b=c for n=4. Points: (1,1) sum=2, (1,2) sum=3, (1,3) sum=4, (1,4) sum=5, (2,1) sum=3, (2,2) sum=4, (2,3) sum=5, (3,1) sum=4, (3,2) sum=5, (4,1) sum=5. So a+2b values: c=3: (1,1) only; c=4: (2,1) only? Actually a+2b=4 => possibilities: b=1 => a=2; b=2 => a=0 invalid; so (2,1) only. c=5: b=1 => a=3; b=2 => a=1; both valid: (3,1) and (1,2) => two points. c=6: b=1 => a=4; b=2 => a=2; b=3 => a=0 invalid; (4,1) and (2,2) => two points. c=7: b=1 => a=5 invalid; b=2 => a=3 => (3,2) sum=5 ok; b=3 => a=1 => (1,3) sum=4 ok; two points. c=8: b=2 => a=4 => (4,2) sum=6 >5 invalid; b=3 => a=2 => (2,3) sum=5 ok; b=4 => a=0 invalid; one point. c=9: b=3 => a=3 => (3,3) sum=6 >5 invalid; b=4 => a=1 => (1,4) sum=5 ok; one point. So max points = 2, matches bound.\n\nThus Lemma 1 seems correct.\n\nLemma 2: Upper bound for number of sunny lines: N_n ≤ (n-k)n + k s_n. Since non-sunny lines can contain at most n points each. Is that correct? Let's examine: Non-sunny lines are horizontal, vertical, or slope -1. Each such line can contain at most n points of the set P_n? Let's verify. For horizontal lines y = b0 (b0 integer). Points (a,b0) with a+b ≤ n+1 => a ≤ n+1 - b0. Since a ≥ 1, there are at most n points? Actually b0 can be from 1 to n (since b positive integer and a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n). For each b0, the number of points on that horizontal line is the number of a such that 1 ≤ a ≤ n+1 - b0. So max occurs when b0 = 1 => a ≤ n => there are n points (a=1,...,n