a 1 was s_n = floor((n+1)/2). Actually Lemma 1 gave that a sunny line can contain at most floor((n+1)/2) points. That bound is derived from the fact that sum a+b changes by |p+q|, and the range of a+b is n-1 (from 2 to n+1). So the maximum number of points is floor((n-1)/|p+q|) + 1. Let's compute floor((n-1)/|p+q|) + 1 = floor((2m)/|p+q|) + 1. For this to be ≤ floor((n+1)/2) = floor((2m+2)/2) = m+1. So we have floor(2m/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ m+1 => floor(2m/|p+q|) ≤ m => 2m/|p+q| < m+1 => |p+q| > 2m/(m+1). So the bound is that |p+q| > 2m/(m+1). Since |p+q| is integer, minimal possible is floor? Actually we need to satisfy floor(2m/|p+q|) ≤ m. For any integer |p+q|≥2, floor(2m/|p+q|) ≤ m because 2m/2 = m, floor = m. For |p+q|=1, floor(2m/1) = 2m > m (for m≥1). So that would exceed bound. So indeed |p+q| cannot be 1 for a sunny line to have at most s_n points; actually for |p+q|=1, the bound would be floor(2m/1) + 1 = 2m+1 > m+1 for m≥2. So that would exceed s_n, but the bound is an upper bound, not a lower bound. Actually Lemma 1 says a sunny line can contain at most floor((n+1)/2) points. If |p+q|=1, the bound from sum argument would be floor(2m/1)+1 = 2m+1 = n+? Actually n=2m+1, so 2m+1 = n. That's larger than s_n = m+1. But the bound from Lemma 1 is the maximum possible; if |p+q|=1, the line could contain up to n points? But does the region allow that many? Let's test with n=5, |p+q|=1 direction (1,0) is horizontal (p+q=1?), but horizontal is not sunny because q=0 (p+q=1 but q=0 violates p,q nonzero condition). Actually sunny lines require p≠0, q≠0, p+q≠0. So direction (1,0) is not allowed because q=0. Direction (1,-2) has p+q = -1, absolute value 1. Let's test line with direction (1,-2). Starting from some point (a,b) in region, moving by (1,-2) reduces sum a+b by 1 each step (since p+q = -1). So sum decreases by 1 each step. So you could potentially have many points as you go downwards. But the region's lower bound for sum is 2. So you could have up to (n+1 - 2)/1 + 1 = n