in \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny ones for every odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) can be placed on three distinct\n   sunny lines, e.g.\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\; y=-3x+5 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\) (Lemma 5).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound obtained in Lemma 2, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1\\quad (n\\ge4),\\qquad\\text{or }0\\le k\\le n\\ (n=3).\n   \\]\n\n   Start from the construction of Lemma 4, which gives a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (for \\(n=3\\) we\n   already have \\(n\\) sunny lines).  \n   Replace arbitrarily \\(n-k\\) of the sunny lines by distinct horizontal\n   lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n   Each replacement turns a sunny line into a non‑sunny one; the set of\n   horizontal lines covers all points whose second coordinate belongs to\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not already\n   covered by the remaining sunny lines has its \\(y\\)-coordinate in this set,\n   hence is covered by one of the new horizontal lines.  \n   The resulting family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and now\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore **every** integer \\(k\\) in the ranges stated above can be\n   realised.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nCombining Lemma 2 (general upper bound), Lemma 3 (exclusion of \\(k=n\\) for\nodd \\(n\\ge5\\)), Lemma 4 (explicit constructions attaining the maximal\nnumber of sunny lines) and Lemma 5 (reduction to any smaller \\(k\\)) yields the\ncomplete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
