ssible values of \\(b\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\max\\bigl(1,c-(n+1)\\bigr)\\le b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{c-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\]\n\n   giving exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points when\n   \\(c=n+2\\).  The same count holds for the family \\(2a+b=c\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line contributes at most \\(s_n\\) points, while a\n   non\u2011sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)\u2013axis, the \\(y\\)\u2013axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\)) contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   N_n\\le (n-k)\\,n+k\\,s_n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N_n=n(n+1)/2\\) and the value of \\(s_n\\) from (3) gives\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[1mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma\u202f2.*  \n   For every odd \\(n\\ge5\\) the equality \\(k=n\\) in (6) cannot hold.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   When \\(n=2m+1\\;(m\\ge2)\\) we have \\(s_n=m+1\\) and (6) becomes an equality\n   precisely when  \n\n   * each sunny line contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points, and  \n   * the point\u2011sets of distinct sunny lines are disjoint.\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 a line with \\(m+1\\) points must belong to one of the families\n   (4).  In the family \\(a+2b=c\\) the constant \\(c\\) can be at most \\(n+2\\);\n   the only value that yields \\(m+1\\) points is \\(c=n+2\\).  Similarly, the\n   family \\(2a+b=c\\) yields \\(m+1\\) points only for \\(c=n+2\\).  Consequently\n   at most **two** sunny lines can have \\(m+1\\) points; every other sunny line\n   contains at most \\(m\\) points.  The total number of points covered by the\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines is therefore\n\n   \\[\n   2(m+1)+(n-2)m=n(m+1)- (n-2)<n(m+1)=ns_n=N_n,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting (6).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all odd \\(n\\ge5\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n   Combining Lemma\u202f2 with (7) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge4 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realising every admissible value of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (all \\(n\\ge3\\)).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; a+2b = i+2\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct and sunny (they belong to the family\n   (4)).  For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) set \\(c=a+2b\\).  \n   If \\(c\\le n+1\\) then \\(c=i+2\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and the\n   point lies on \\(L_i\\).  If \\(c=n+2\\) we place the point on the horizontal\n   line  \n\n   \\[\n   H:\\; y=m+1\\quad\\bigl(m:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   All points with \\(c=n+2\\) have the same second coordinate \\(b=m+1\\); thus\n   they are covered by \\(H\\).  The family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1},H\\}\\) consists\n   of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Construction for smaller \\(k\\).*  \n   Starting from the family above, replace arbitrarily \\(n-1-k\\) of the sunny\n   lines \\(L_i\\) by distinct horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1-k\\).  Horizontal lines are non\u2011sunny, and each\n   of them contains all points whose second coordinate equals the chosen\n   value.  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is still covered.\n   The resulting set consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny ones.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is\n   attainable.\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=x,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\; y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct and cover all six points (a direct substitution\n   verifies each point, see the table below).  Thus for \\(n=3\\) we can have\n   \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point}&\\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & \\ell_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & \\ell_1\\\\\n   (1,3) & \\ell_2\\\\\n   (2,1) & \\ell_2\\\\\n   (1,2) & \\ell_3\\\\\n   (3,1) & \\ell_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   By the same replacement trick as above we obtain configurations with any\n   smaller number of sunny lines.\n\n7. **Conclusion**  \n\n   *Upper bound:* Lemma\u202f1 together with the counting argument (6) yields\n   \\(k\\le n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) for \\(n=3\\).  \n   *Sharpness:* the explicit constructions above realise every integer\n   \\(k\\) in the ranges  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;(n\\ge4),\\qquad\n   \\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;(n=3).\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one\n   stated in the boxed answer. \u220e"
