all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence every required point is covered. All the listed lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), so they are non\u2011sunny. Thus \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=n-2\\).**  \n   Keep only the two non\u2011sunny lines used above, namely \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(1\\le d\\le n-2\\) introduce the sunny line  \n   \\[\n   L_d:\\;a-b=d\\qquad\\text{(i.e. }y = x-d\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(L_d\\) is \\(1\\), which is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so each \\(L_d\\) is sunny.  \n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a required point that is **not** on \\(y=1\\) (so \\(b\\ge2\\)) and **not** on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (so \\(a+b\\le n\\)).  \n   Define \\(d=a-b\\). Because \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   1\\le a-b\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(d\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_d\\).  \n   Consequently every required point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{y=1,\\;x+y=n+1,\\;L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\}\\). The construction uses exactly two non\u2011sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, so \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   Combining Lemma\u202f1 with the two explicit constructions we obtain the rigorous inclusions  \n   \\[\n   \\{0,\\;n-2\\}\\subseteq\\{k\\text{ admissible}\\}\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n   The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-3\\) has not been settled in this answer."
