s n points. A sunny line cannot be vertical, so it can intersect this column at at most one point. So to cover all n points in column 1, we need at least n lines that intersect column 1 at distinct points. These lines could be non-sunny (vertical) or sunny. But we have only n lines total. So if we have any non-sunny lines that are vertical, they can cover many points in column 1 (if they are vertical lines x=1). But we cannot have more than one vertical line at x=1 because lines must be distinct. So at most one vertical line can be x=1. So we need at least n-1 other lines that intersect column 1 at distinct points. Those lines must be non-vertical (i.e., not vertical). So they can be sunny, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal lines intersect column 1 at exactly one point each (since they intersect at a specific y). So horizontal lines can also cover points in column 1. Similarly, slope -1 lines intersect column 1 at at most one point (since they intersect each vertical line at most one point). So any line that is not vertical can intersect column 1 at at most one point. So to cover all n points in column 1, we need at least n lines, each intersecting column 1 at a distinct point. Since we have exactly n lines, we must have each line intersect column 1 at a distinct point. That means each line must intersect column 1 at a unique point, and no two lines intersect column 1 at the same point. So we need exactly n lines covering column 1. This is possible only if each line is distinct and each line passes through a distinct point (1,b) for b=1..n. So we need a set of n lines each passing through a distinct point (1,b). Among those lines, some may be vertical (x=1) but only one can be vertical (x=1). The rest must be non-vertical. So we need at least n-1 non-vertical lines. But we have total n lines, so at most one line can be vertical. So we need at least n-1 non-vertical lines. Among non-vertical lines, some could be horizontal (y=some constant) or slope -1 or sunny. So