  \\[\n   \\mathcal D:=\\Bigl\\{d\\in\\mathbb Z\\;\\Big|\\;n-2k\\le d\\le n-3\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal D|=(n-3)-(n-2k)+1=2k-2\\le k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Choose any set of \\(k\\) distinct integers \\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\) that contains \\(\\mathcal D\\) (possible by (6)).  \n   For each \\(d_j\\) add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{d_j}:\\;y=x-d_j\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   1. If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H\\).  \n   2. If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V\\).  \n   3. If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D\\).  \n   4. If \\(2\\le a\\le m-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\) (the extra vertical line).  \n   5. Otherwise \\(a\\in\\{1,m-1,m,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(b\\neq1\\), \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  \n      In this situation the **difference** \\(d:=a-b\\) satisfies  \n\n      \\[\n      n-2k\\le d\\le n-3,\n      \\]\n\n      i.e. \\(d\\in\\mathcal D\\subseteq\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\).  \n      Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{d}\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(m=n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) is attainable.\n\n4. **Small case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) consists of the six points  \n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n   The three non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\n   \\]\n\n   cover all six points, giving \\(k=0\\).  \n   Replacing the diagonal by the sunny line \\(y=x\\) yields a family with exactly one sunny line, so \\(k=1\\) is also possible.  No configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists because Lemma\u202f1 forces at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 provides the necessary condition \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) (and \\(k\\le1\\) for \\(n=3\\)).  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying this condition can indeed be realised.  \n   Therefore the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the verdict. \u220e"
