rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (necessary condition)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nWrite \\(m=n-k\\) for the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Maximum points covered by the non‑sunny lines.*  \nThe three longest non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nH:y=1,\\qquad V:x=1,\\qquad D:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nTheir pairwise intersections are the three corner points \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\); consequently they together cover  \n\n\\[\n3n-3=3(n-1)\n\\]\n\ndistinct points.\n\nAny further non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families (horizontal, vertical, diagonal).  \nSuch a line meets each of \\(H,V,D\\) in a point already counted, so it can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) new points.  \nHence for every \\(m\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{ns}}(m)=3n-3+(m-3)(n-1)=m(n-1)      \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points are covered by the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n(The same formula gives the correct values \\(C_{\\text{ns}}(1)=n\\) and \\(C_{\\text{ns}}(2)=2n-1\\).)\n\n*Points contributed by the sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line can intersect each non‑sunny line in at most one point, therefore it can add at most \\(m\\) new points that are not already on a non‑sunny line.  \nTwo distinct sunny lines intersect in at most one point, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can add at most  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{s}}(k,m)=km+\\binom{k}{2}      \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nnew points.\n\n*Counting.*  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered, thus\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le C_{\\text{ns}}(m)+C_{\\text{s}}(k,m).      \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(m=n-k\\) and (1)–(2) into (3):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)(n-1)+k(n-k)+\\binom{k}{2}\n               =n(n-1)+\\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{k^{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging gives\n\n\\[\nk^{2}-k\\le n^{2}-3n.      \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the left–hand side is a convex quadratic in \\(k\\), inequality (4) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}.      \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (sufficiency)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nSet \\(m=n-k\\) (the required number of non‑sunny lines).\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nH:y=1,\\qquad V:x=1,\\qquad D:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m>3\\) (i.e. \\(k<n-3\\)), add the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,m-2).\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nA direct count shows that they cover  \n\n\\[\nC_{\\text{ns}}(m)=m(n-1)\n\\]\n\ndifferent points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the same number as in (1)).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nConsider the integer interval  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I:=\\{d\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid n-2k\\le d\\le n-3\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal I|=(n-3)-(n-2k)+1=2k-2\\le k .\n\\]\n\nChoose any set \\(\\{d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k}\\}\\) of \\(k\\) distinct integers that contains \\(\\mathcal I\\) (add arbitrary extra integers if necessary).  \nFor each \\(d_{j}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{d_{j}}:\\;y=x-d_{j}\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n**Coverage proof.**  \nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(V\\).  \n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on \\(D\\).  \n*If \\(2\\le a\\le m-2\\)*, the point lies on the extra vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).  \n\nOtherwise \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,m-1,m,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and we have \\(b\\neq1\\) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\).  \nIn this situation the **difference** \\(d:=a-b\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nn-2k\\le d\\le n-3 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\ge a-(n+1-a)=2a-n-1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nd=a-b\\le a-(2a-n-1)=n+1-a\\le n-3,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a\\ge m-1=n-k-1\\ge n/2\\).  \nSimilarly, \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) gives \\(d\\ge a-(n+1-a)=2a-n-1\\ge n-2k\\).  \nThus \\(d\\in\\mathcal I\\subseteq\\{d_{1},\\dots ,d_{k}\\}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{d}\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  \nThe construction uses exactly \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Small case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=1\\).  \nThe three non‑sunny lines \\(y=1,\\;x=1,\\;x+y=4\\) cover all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\) (giving \\(k=0\\)).  \nReplacing the diagonal by the sunny line \\(y=x\\) yields a configuration with exactly one sunny line (\\(k=1\\)).  \nNo configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists because Lemma 1 gives the bound \\(k\\le1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion\n\nLemma 1 proves that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3