nny lines. We can choose any three non-sunny lines: maybe x=1, y=1, and x=2 (vertical). Then uncovered points: after x=1 and y=1, we have (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2). Then x=2 covers (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). So left: (3,2),(3,3),(4,2). That's three points again. Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Same issue: (3,2)-(4,2) horizontal (non-sunny), (3,2)-(3,3) vertical (non-sunny), (3,3)-(4,2) slope -1 (non-sunny). So each sunny line can cover at most one. So need 3 sunny lines. So k=2 not possible with that selection.\n\nMaybe we need to use different non-sunny lines that are not axis-aligned but diagonal x+y=6? Already tried that. Could we use diagonal x+y=5? That line includes points where a+b=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). That might help cover some of the leftover points. Let's try to choose non-sunny lines: x=1, y=1, diagonal x+y=5. Then uncovered after x=1 and y=1: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,2),(3,3),(4,2). Diagonal x+y=5 covers (1,4) (already covered by x=1?), (2,3),(3,2),(4,1). So after adding diagonal, uncovered: (2,2),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2). That's 4 points. Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Could a sunny line cover two of these? Let's check pairs: (2,2)-(2,4) vertical (non-sunny). (2,2)-(3,3) slope 1 (sunny!). So a sunny line y=x passes through (2,2),(3,3). Also (2,4)-(4,2) slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny). (2,4)-(4,2) slope -1, not allowed. (2,2)-(4,2) horizontal (non-sunny). (3,3)-(4,2) slope -1 (non-sunny). So we can cover (2,2) and (3,3) with sunny line y=x. Then left points: (2,4),(4,2). These two points have slope -1 line (non-sunny). So we cannot cover both with a sunny line. Could we use a different selection of non-sunny lines to allow better grouping? Perhaps use x=1, vertical x=2, diagonal x+y=5? Let's try: Non-sunny: x=1, x=2, diagonal x+y=5. Then covered: x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,4). x=2 covers (2,1)-(2,4). diagonal x+y=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). After these, uncovered points: (3,1) (covered by y=1? Actually we didn't include y=1 as non-sunny; we have only 3 non-sunny lines: x=1, x=2, diagonal. So we need to cover all points with 2 sunny lines as well. Let's list all points for n=5: (a,b) with a,b≥1, a+b≤6.\n\nList all points:\na=1: b=1..5 => (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5)\na=2: b=1..4 => (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\na=3: b=1..3 => (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\na=4: b=1..2 => (4,1),(4,2)\na=5: b=1 => (5,1)\n\nNow cover with x=1: covers (1,1)-(1,5). x=2: covers (2,1)-(2,4). diagonal x+y=5: covers points where a+b=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). So after these, uncovered points: (3,1),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Also maybe (2,2) is covered? (2,2) not covered by x=1 or x=2 or diagonal (since a+b=4). So (2,2) is uncovered. Also (3,2) is covered by diagonal. (4,1) covered by diagonal. (5,1) uncovered. So uncovered set: (2,2),(3,1),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points. Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. That's impossible because each sunny line can at most cover some of them. Let's see if any sunny line can cover more than one of these points. Check pairs:\n\n- (2,2)-(3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1 (non-sunny)\n- (2,2)-(3,3): slope (3-2)/(3-2) = 1 (sunny). So line y = x passes through (2,2),(3,3). Also passes through (4,4) but not in set.\n- (2,2)-(4,2): slope 0 (non-sunny)\n- (2,2)-(5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 (sunny). So line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope -1/3 is sunny.\n- (3,1)-(3,3): vertical (non-sunny)\n- (3,1)-(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-3) = 1 (sunny). So line y = x - 2? Actually passes through (3,1) and (4,2) slope 1.\n- (3,1)-(5,1): slope 0 (non-sunny)\n- (3,3)-(4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-3) = -1 (non-sunny)\n- (3,3)-(5,1): slope (1-3)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1 (non-sunny)\n- (4,2)-(5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-4) = -1 (non-sunny)\n\nThus we can try to cover with two sunny lines: maybe line L1: y = x passes through (2,2),(3,3). L2: y = x - 2 passes through (3,1),(4,2). That covers (2,2),(3,3),(3,1),(4,2). Left uncovered: (5,1) and (2,2) already covered, (5,1) not covered. Could we adjust L2 to also cover (5,1)? Let's see line through (3,1) and (5,1) is hor