a line through (1,4) with slope -2: y = -2x + 6, which also passes through (2,2). Good. So L1: y = -2x + 6 covers (1,4) and (2,2). L2: keep as y = x + 1 covering (2,3) and (1,2). L3: keep as y = x - 1 covering (3,2) and (2,1). L4: maybe use a line through (4,1) with slope -2: y = -2x + 9 (since 1 = -2*4 + b => b=9). This passes through (3,3) (outside), (2,5) (outside). So only (4,1). Not covering missing points.\n\nNow covered: (1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(1,2),(3,2),(2,1),(4,1). Missing (1,1),(1,3),(3,1). Could we adjust L4 to cover (1,3) or (3,1)? Let's try a line through (4,1) with slope -0.5: y = -0.5x + b => 1 = -0.5*4 + b => b=3 => line y = -0.5x + 3. This passes through (2,2) (already covered), (1,2.5) (not integer), (3,1.5) (not integer). So not covering missing integer points.\n\nTry slope -1/3: y = -(1/3)x + b => 1 = -(1/3)*4 + b => b = 1 + 4/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. This passes through (3,2) (since -(1/3)*3 + 7/3 = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333, not integer). So not helpful.\n\nTry slope -3: y = -3x + b => 1 = -3*4 + b => b = 13 => line y = -3x + 13 passes through (3,4) (outside), (2,7) (outside). So not helpful.\n\nTry slope 0? Not allowed (horizontal). Slope ∞? Not allowed (vertical). Slope 2: y = 2x + b => 1 = 2*4 + b => b = -7 => line y = 2x -7 passes through (3,-1) (outside), (2,-3) (outside). Not helpful.\n\nThus L4 cannot cover missing points. Perhaps we can adjust L2 or L3 to cover missing points. Let's try L2 through (2,3) with slope -2: y = -2x + b => 3 = -2*2 + b => b = 7 => line y = -2x + 7 passes through (3,1) (since -2*3+7=1). So L2 would cover (2,3) and (3,1). Good. Then we have L1: y = -2x + 6 covering (1,4) and (2,2). L2: y = -2x + 7 covering (2,3) and (3,1). L3: maybe keep y = x - 1 covering (3,2) and (2,1). L4: we need to cover (4,1) and maybe (1,3) or (1,1). Let's try L4 through (4,1) with slope -1? Not allowed. Slope -2: y = -2x + b => 1 = -2*4 + b => b = 9 => line y = -2x + 9 passes through (3,3) (outside), (2,5) (outside). So not covering missing points. Sl