non‑sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   The points of the “diagonal’’  \n   \\[\n   D_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   are exactly \\(n\\) points.  \n   Any sunny line has slope \\(\\neq-1\\) and therefore meets the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one point; consequently a sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_n\\).  \n   A non‑sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(D_n\\) only if it is the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) itself (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, giving the universal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Direct inspection shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible: with two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line we cannot cover the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) (each sunny line can contain at most one of them, while the single non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or the diagonal, none of which covers more than one of the three).  \n   The configurations with \\(k=0\\) (three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\)) and with \\(k=1\\) (non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=x\\)) both work.  \n   Thus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(n\\ge 4\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=n-1-k\\qquad(\\text{so }0\\le m\\le n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Use the following \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &D:\\;x+y=n+1,\\\\\n   &V_t:\\;x=t\\qquad (t=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(D\\) contains all points of the diagonal \\(D_n\\).  \n   Each vertical line \\(V_t\\) contains exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(t\\); in particular \\(V_t\\) covers the points \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = x-(k+1-i).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny, and they are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (2)–(3).*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(a+b=n+1\\)**, then \\((a,b)\\in D\\).\n\n   2. **If \\(a\\le m\\)**, then \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\) (the vertical line with \\(t=a\\)).\n\n   3. **Otherwise \\(a\\ge m+1=n-k\\).**  \n      From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k+1\\).  \n      Hence \\(0\\le a-b\\le k\\).  \n      Write \\(a-b = k+1-i\\) for the unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \n      By construction this equality means \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\n   In every possible case the point belongs to at least one of the chosen lines, so the family of \\(n\\) lines indeed covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(V_t\\)’s together with \\(D\\)).  All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, the diagonal line has slope \\(-1\\), and the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\).\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and \\(n\\ge4\\)) a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the universal upper bound (1) with the explicit constructions, we obtain:\n\n   * for \\(n=3\\): admissible \\(k\\) are \\(0,1\\);\n   * for every \\(n\\ge4\\): admissible \\(k\\) are all integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n   Hence the complete answer is the boxed statement above.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show 