f its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is *sunny*.\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   The points of the outer diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Any sunny line meets \\(D_n\\) in at most one point, therefore at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Direct inspection shows that three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by three distinct lines containing two sunny lines; consequently \\(k=2\\) is impossible. Configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are exhibited, giving the admissible set \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n4. **Construction for all \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   We give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Base line (always present):* the non‑sunny diagonal  \n   \\[\n   L_D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = m_i x + c_i ,\\qquad \n   m_i:=\\frac{i-1}{\\,n-2i+1\\,},\\qquad \n   c_i:=1-m_i i .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(\\,n+1-i,\\,i\\,) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) (the denominator in \\(m_i\\) never vanishes and \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) would imply \\(i=n\\), which is excluded), each \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   *Coverage verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in L_D\\).\n\n   *If \\(a+b\\le n\\)*, set \\(i=a\\).  \n   Because \\(b\\le n-a\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-i-1\\); consequently \\(b\\ge 1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\). Indeed, using (2) we compute  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i a + c_i\n   =\\frac{i-1}{\\,n-2i+1\\,}a+1-\\frac{i-1}{\\,n-2i+1\\,}i\n   =\\frac{(i-1)(a-i)}{\\,n-2i+1\\,}+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a=i\\), the fraction vanishes and the right–hand side equals \\(1\\).  \n   For any \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = 1+\\frac{(i-1)(a-i)}{\\,n-2i+1\\,}= m_i a + c_i ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\). Hence every point with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\).\n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_n=\\{L_D\\}\\cup\\{L_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains **exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines**, and covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). This proves the existence for \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n5. **Obtaining fewer sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) (which has \\(k=n-1\\)), we may replace any sunny line \\(L_i\\) by the non‑sunny vertical line \\(x=i\\).  \n   The vertical line \\(x=i\\) still contains the point \\(P_i=(i,1)\\) and, because the diagonal \\(L_D\\) already covers the diagonal point \\(Q_i\\), the replacement does not destroy coverage.  \n   By performing this replacement for exactly \\(n-1-k\\) different indices we obtain a family of \\(n\\) lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The universal bound (1) together with the explicit constructions for \\(k=n-1\\) and the reduction procedure for smaller \\(k\\) shows that for every \\(n\\ge4\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are attainable. Together with the separate analysis of \\(n=3\\) we obtain the final answer displayed above.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully 