e points (since a line can intersect column-1 at one point and bottom row at one point). So we need at least ceil(7/2)=4 lines. So 4 sunny lines is exactly enough. So we need to find 4 sunny lines each covering a distinct column-1 point and a distinct bottom point (or maybe covering two column-1 points? But a sunny line cannot be vertical, so can intersect column-1 at at most one point. Similarly cannot be horizontal, so can intersect bottom row at at most one point. So each sunny line can cover at most one column-1 point and at most one bottom point. So each sunny line can cover at most 2 points among those 7. So we need exactly 4 lines covering 7 points; one line will cover only one point (maybe a column-1 point). But we need to cover all points. Let's try to assign a derangement.\n\nSet of column-1 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Set of bottom points: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). We have 4 sunny lines. We could assign each column-1 point to a line; three of those lines also cover a bottom point (distinct). The remaining column-1 point is covered by a line that doesn't cover any bottom point (maybe passes through some other point not needed). So we need a matching between bottom points and column-1 points except one column-1 point left unmatched. That's possible.\n\nSpecifically, we can pair (2,1) with (1,3) (line through (2,1) and (1,3) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, which is not -1, 0, infinite => sunny). Pair (3,1) with (1,2) (slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny). Pair (4,1) with (1,4) (slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed!). So can't pair (4,1) with (1,4). Use (1,1) maybe? (4,1) with (1,1) slope = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, not allowed. So we need to pair (4,1) with (1,2) maybe? But (1,2) already used. Let's try different pairing: (2,1) with (1,4) slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. (3,1) with (1,3) slope = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't use (1,3). (3,1) with (1,2) slope = -1/2, okay. (4,1) with (1,1) slope = 0, not allowed. So we need to pair (4,1) with (1,3) slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, okay. So we have assignments: (2,1)-(1,4), (3,1)-(1,2), (4,1)-(1,3). That uses column-1 points (1,4),(1,2),(1,3). Leaves (1,1) uncovered. So we need a line covering (1,1) and possibly other points not needed. Could be line through (1,1) and (2,2) maybe? But (2,2) is not yet covered. Actually (2,2) is not on diagonal (since 2+2=4 <=6). So we need to cover (2,2) as well. So we need to ensure all points are covered. Let's list all points not yet covered after using diagonal line and three sunny lines:\n\nDiagonal line covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Sunny lines we propose:\n\nLine L1: through (2,1) and (1,4). This line also passes through maybe (3,? )? Let's compute equation: slope -3, passes through (2,1): y-1 = -3(x-2) => y = -3x + 7. At x=3, y= -9+7 = -2 (not positive). So only covers (2,1) and (1,4). Good.\n\nLine L2: through (3,1) and (1,2): slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, equation: y-1 = -1/2(x-3) => y = -1/2 x + 5/2. At x=2, y = -1 + 5/2 = 3/2 (not integer). At x=4, y = -2 + 5/2 = 1/2 (not integer). So only covers (3,1) and (1,2). Good.\n\nLine L3: through (4,1) and (1,3): slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, equation: y-1 = -2/3(x-4) => y = -2/3 x + 8/3 + 1 = -2/3 x + 11/3? Wait compute: y-1 = -2/3(x-4) => y = 1 -2/3 x + 8/3 = (3/3) + (8/3) - (2/3)x = (11/3) - (2/3)x. At x=2, y = 11/3 - 4/3 = 7/3 (not integer). At x=3, y = 11/3 - 6/3 = 5/3. So only covers (4,1) and (1,3). Good.\n\nNow we still need to cover (1,1) and (2,2) and (3,2) and (2,3) and (1,2) is covered, (1,3) covered, (1,4) covered, (1,5) covered, (2,1) covered, (3,1) covered, (4,1) covered, (5,1) covered. Points left: (2,2), (3,2), (2,3), (3,2) already listed, (2,3) also. (3,2) and (2,3) are symmetric. Also (2,2) is left. Also (3,2) and (2,3) are not covered. Also (3,2) is not on diagonal (3+2=5 <=6). So we need to cover these with the remaining sunny line (the 4th sunny line). That line can cover at most 2 points (if it passes through a column-1 point and a bottom point). But we have no column-1 points left (only (1,1) left). So we could use line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, which is not 0, infinite, -1, so sunny. That line also passes through (3,3) which is on diagonal (already covered). So L4: line y = x (through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4? but (4,4) not in set because 4+4=8>6). So L4 covers (1,1),(2,2) and also (3,3) (already covered). So after L4, we still have (2,3) and (3,2) uncovered. But we have used all 5 lines (diagonal + 4 sunny). So we didn't cover all points. So that construction fails.\n\nWe could try different assignment of lines. Perhaps we can use a different non-sunny line, not diagonal, maybe horizontal or vertical. But we need exactly one non-sunny line. Could be vertical x=1 covering column-1 points. But that would be non-sunny vertical line covering (1,1)-(1,5). Then we need to cover the rest with 4 sunny lines. Let's try that.\n\nTake non-sunny line V1: x=1 covering (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). Then we need to cover remaining points: bottom row (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1) and interior points (2,2),(3,2),(2,3),(3,3),(4,2),(2,4),(3,3) etc. Actually we need to list all points not on column 1. Points are:\n\nRow 1: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1)\nRow 2: (2,2),(3,2),(4,2)\nRow 3: (2,3),(3,3)\nRow 4: (2,4)\nRow 5: none (since (1,5) is column 1). Also (5,1) is bottom row.\n\nTotal remaining points: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1) = 4; (2,2),(3,2),(4,2) = 3; (2,3),(3,3) = 2; (2,4) = 1. So 10 points left.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines left to cover 10 points. Each sunny line can cover many points if they are collinear. We need to find 4 sunny lines covering all these points. Is that possible? Let's think.\n\nWe can try to use lines through (2,1)-(3,2)-(4,3?) but (4,3) not in set because 4+3=7>6. So maybe lines like y = x - 1 (slope 1) passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) not in set, (5,4?) not in set. So line y = x - 1 covers (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) no. So it covers (2,1) and (3,2). That's two points.\n\nLine y = x - 2 passes through (3,1),(4,2),(5,3?) not in set. So covers (3,1),(4,2). Good.\n\nLine y = x - 3 passes through (4,1),(5,2?) not in set. So covers (4,1) only.\n\nLine y = x - 4 passes through (5,1) only.\n\nWe still need to cover (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). Could use vertical line x=2 (non-sunny) but we only have sunny lines left. So we need to incorporate those points into sunny lines. Maybe use line through (2,2) and (5,1): slope = (2-1)/(2-5) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, sunny. That line also passes through (3,? )? At x=3, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(3-2) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3, not integer. So only covers (2,2) and (5,1). Good.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (5,1): slope = (3-1)/(2-5) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, sunny. That line also passes through (3,? )? At x=3, y = 3 + (-2/3)*(3-2) = 3 - 2/3 = 7/3, not integer. So covers (2,3) and (5,1) (but (5,1) already covered by previous line maybe). So we need to coordinate.\n\nWe can try to use line through (2,4) and (5,1): slope = (4-1)/(2-5) = 3/(-3) = -1, not allowed (slope -1). So cannot use that. So (2,4) cannot be paired with (5,1) in a sunny line. Could pair (2,4) with (4,1): slope = (4-1)/(2-4) = 3/(-2) = -3/2, sunny. That line also passes through (3,? )? At x=3, y = 4 + (-3/2)*(3-2) = 4 - 3/2 = 5/2, not integer. So covers (2,4) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,2),(2,3) maybe with other lines.\n\nWe have used lines: L1: y = x - 1 (covers (2,1),(3,2)); L2: y = x - 2 (covers (3,1),(4,2)); L3: line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope -1/3; L4: line through (2,4) and (4,1) slope -3/2. That's 4 sunny lines. Let's check coverage:\n\n- (2,1) covered by L1.\n- (3,2) covered by L1.\n- (3,1) covered by L2.\n- (4,2) covered by L2.\n- (5,1) covered by line through (2,2) and (5,1) (L3).\n- (2,2) covered by L3.\n- (2,4) covered by L4.\n- (4,1) covered by L4.\n- (2,3) not covered yet.\n- (3,1) covered.\n- (3,2) covered.\n- (4,2) covered.\n- (5,1) covered.\n- (2,3) remains uncovered.\n- (3,3) is on diagonal? Actually (3,3) sum = 6, which is <=6, not on diagonal x+y=6? Wait diagonal is x+y=6? For n=5, diagonal line is x+y=6 (since n+1=6). So (3,3) is on diagonal, which is covered by the diagonal line? Wait we used vertical line x=1 as non-sunny line, not diagonal. So (3,3) is not covered yet. So we need to cover (3,3) as well. So we have uncovered points (2,3) and (3,3). That's two points left, but we have used all 4 sunny lines. So this fails.\n\nWe could try a different non-sunny line: maybe use diagonal line x+y=6 as non-sunny, which covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Then we need to cover the rest with 4 sunny lines. The rest are column-1 points (1,1)-(1,4) and bottom points (2,1)-(4,1). That's 7 points. As we saw, we need to cover 7 points with 4 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most two of these points (one column-1 and one bottom). So we need to cover 7 points with 4 lines, which is possible if one line covers only one point. So we need to find a matching that covers 6 points in pairs, leaving one column-1 point alone. That is possible if we can find a derangement for 3 bottom points and 3 column-1 points, leaving one column-1 point unmatched. Let's try to find such assignment.\n\nWe have column-1 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Bottom points: (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). We need to pair three bottom points with three distinct column-1 points (not (1,1) because that gives slope 0). So we need to assign b values from {2,3,4} to bottom points i=2,3,4 such that b != i. That's a derangement of size 3 on set {2,3,4}. Derangements of size 3 exist: e.g., mapping 2->3, 3->4, 4->2. So we can assign:\n\n- (2,1) with (1,3): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny.\n- (3,1) with (1,4): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2, sunny.\n- (4,1) with (1,2): slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, sunny.\n\nNow we have used column-1 points (1,3),(1,4),(1,2). Left column-1 point (1,1) uncovered. We need a sunny line covering (1,1). It can also cover some other point not yet covered, but all other points are already covered: bottom points are covered, column-1 points (1,2),(1,3),(1,4) are covered, diagonal points are covered. So we need a line covering (1,1) and maybe some other point already covered (that's okay). For example, line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = 1, sunny. Does (2,2) need coverage? (2,2) is not on diagonal (2+2=4 <=6) and not covered yet. So we need to cover (2,2). So we can use line through (1,1) and (2,2) which also passes through (3,3) which is on diagonal (already covered). So that line covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). So all points are covered. So we have 4 sunny lines: L1: (2,1)-(1,3), L2: (3,1)-(1,4), L3: (4,1)-(1,2), L4: (1,1)-(2,2). And one non-sunny line: diagonal x+y=6. That's 5 distinct lines. Are they distinct? Yes, each line is distinct. So we have a configuration with k=4 sunny lines for n=5, i.e., k=n-1. So the solution's claim that k=n-1 is impossible for n>=5 is false. Indeed we just constructed a configuration for n=5 with k=4. So the answer given is wrong. Let's double-check that all points are covered.\n\nList points:\n\n- Diagonal line (non-sunny): (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\n\n- Sunny line L1: through (2,1) and (1,3). Equation: slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. So line: y-1 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 5. Check points: (2,1) works, (1,3) works. Does it also cover any other points? At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (no). So covers only those two.\n\n- Sunny line L2: through (3,1) and (1,4). Slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2. Equation: y-1 = -3/2 (x-3) => y = -3/2 x + 5/2 + 1 = -3/2 x + 7/2. At x=2, y = -3 + 7/2 = 1/2 (no). At x=4, y = -6 + 7/2 = -5/2 (no). So only those two.\n\n- Sunny line L3: through (4,1) and (1,2). Slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3. Equation: y-1 = -1/3 (x-4) => y = -1/3 x + 4/3 + 1 = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 (no). At x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 (no). So only those two.\n\n- Sunny line L4: through (1,1) and (2,2). Slope = 1. Equation: y = x. This line also passes through (3,3) (already covered by diagonal) and (4,4) (not in set). So covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). (3,3) is already covered, but that's fine.\n\nNow check all points in P5:\n\nRow